CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, profound psychomotor retardation, severe cognitive slowing, and somatic lethargy resulting entirely from complex psychosocial exhaustion (intersectional fatigue). Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was critical to perform a full neurological exam to definitively exclude an acute organic cause for the sudden decline in consciousness/alertness, such as an atypical infection, metabolic crash, or silent delirium. Delaying care until routine hours would have risked missing a critical organic pathology masked by the psychological presentation, firmly justifying the necessity for urgent after-hours medical evaluation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

